A||||9||RFMike||Help.  Mickey, I've been reaped.
Z||000000||RFMike||06-23-99||06:15 PM||||Don't know if anybody remembers that old joke, but that's how I feel.  Since I've bought this car, I've had gravel spun up on it, keyed, the antenna broken, and today, I got gravelled again.  That wasn't enough, so the &*^%$% drove into the front of it, left a crease in the nose, pushed the hood back 1", and the light covers are sprung.  If anybody knows how to get the hood open when the release won't function, I'd love to hear from you.  Course, it might be awhile before I get to it.  I may camp out at the parking lot until I find somebody with a high bumper with black paint on the underside.<br>Seriously, I need to get at this and at least get the covers down.<br>Thanks<br>Mike
Z||000001||87GT||06-23-99||07:31 PM||||I just bought my 87GT recently and havent had problems like that at all!  I cant help you with the hood, but I do hope things get a little better for you.  
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||06-23-99||07:57 PM||||RFMike,<p>I hope you catch whoever did this. Don`t know about the hood but my car has been hit twice in the last two months. I keep one of those little fun saver cameras in my car at all times now. Some times I will take a picture of a car parked next to me (front or back). If it looks to close. And that is how I cought the lady who hit me the last time.<br>Hope all turns out ok.
Z||000003||theogre||06-23-99||09:59 PM||||where in hell do you live? Remind me I Nver want to got there.<p>I had to think about this one a bit...... I don't think what's holding your hood down is the latch.<p>On the rear corners of the hood there are 2 pockets. When the hood is closed these 2 pockets sit over 2 large headed bolts. If the hood is pushed back in an impact, it is the job of these pockets to catch the hood and keep it from comming thru the windscreen. This safety device is used on all front opening hoods sold in the US since I can't remember how long ago.<p>If the hood is back a whole inch, I'd bet it's caught the bolts in the pockets. There is no way you will open it unless you can shove it forward. If you can slip a wrench downt the front, you may be able to get the lower hinge bolts off. If you can't get the bolts off, you might have to cut the hinges, or worse, damage/destroy the hood.<p>The 2 safety bolts are a large Torx drive. You could maybe drill a whole in the hood over them and unscrew them from the car. This of course means you have 2 holes to fill, but that is assuming the hood was salvagable in the first place. I'll try to dimension the pocket tomorow to narrow down your drilling location.<p>To test this for sure, have someone hold the release, and pull on a corner of the hood. If it's the safety catches, the corners will not move. Even with the center latch locked, you can usually pull up the corners a bit. The hood is pretty flexible. If with the release held, you can flext it up a bit in the back center, its definatly the safetys.
Z||000004||lowCG||06-24-99||01:01 AM||||That sucks,reminds me of a time when I'd painted my car earlier that weekend,went to show it off to a friend,went to a pub,came out,and the nose was gone, stolen.<br>Far out.
Z||000005||RFMike||06-24-99||04:58 AM||||Ogre, I live in Wisconsin, work in Minnesota.  I park in downtown Minneapolis.  The trendy architectural firm I work for has an office in the warehouse district....  back to the real issue.<br>The hood is definitely latched into the safety pockets.  The hood appears undamaged, not so for the nose.  I'm thinking I'll take off the nose and hook a come-along to the hinges, if I can get at them, or something else solid.  If it slid back, it should slide forward.  Don't want to drill if i don't have to.<br>Every time I go out to the garage to work on my baby, I come back foaming at the mouth again.  Probably never catch this @#$%, but if I do....
Z||000006||Racer 1||06-24-99||03:17 PM||||Well I have two solutions to your problem RFMIKE.First install in the rear trunk compartment a huge bomb with a mercury switch to set it off.Then the next time someone high sides your car there get more then a scratch on there car.And for your hood problem back in the early-late 1980's chainsaws were popular as weapons in killer moves.They cut open just about everything this might work on your fiero's hood and you could get crafty with the car that might-a- hit you if you found him.Just remeber to wear saftey glasses with the chainsaw fiberglass isnt fun in the eye's..... GOOD LUCK....   8-)
Z||000007||lowCG||06-25-99||01:14 AM||||I've had the noses of two cars removed on three different,unfortunate occasions,and I've begun to attribute this to Black Car Syndrome(BCS).<br>If you go pulling on the front of your car,be sure and do something to spread out the tugging force so as not to stretch out the metal that's there and make things harder to put back together.Even if you have to weld something onto the panels,like thicker pieces of metal.I used a tree,and a very steep driveway to much success;drive up,coast back,clunk!
Z||000008||Bozone||06-25-99||09:29 PM||||I had that problem with my 86. The hood wedged against those pins and wouldn't free. I hooked a flat hook behind the hood, tied it to a tree and backed up. All it did was pull the plastic off the back. Finally sabersawed the hood until I could get a crowbar in there. The hood was wrecked anyway. So drilling holes above the pins IS a good option if the hood is worth saving. 
Z||000009||RFMike||06-26-99||12:28 PM||||After grunting, moaning, and groaning, a body man and I got the nose off and the hinge plates disconnected.  The front end is free, but it is still caught in the back.  My buddy thinks there is no safety catch, and that the latch pins are jambed in the holes.  The latch cable seems to free it, but it won't come up beyond the latch point.  I don't think taking the fender off will help, but am going to try it anyway.  The hood is undamaged (cosmetically- the hinge plates are bent, probably other stuff as well), so I don't want to drill it if i don't have to.  Anybody have any suggestions?
